## IQA Service

The IQA (Image Quality Analysis) service is used to evaluate the quality of images captured during the OCR and Document Liveness processes. It helps ensure that the images meet certain quality standards before they are processed further.

In order to enable IQA service, you need to set the `.iqaEnabled(boolean)` property to `true` in the `CardRecognizerCredentials` builder when initializing the OCR.

```java
@Override
public CardRecognizerCredentials getCredentials() {
    CardRecognizerCredentials credentials = new CardRecognizerCredentials.Builder()
        ...
        .iqaEnabled(true)
        .build();
    return credentials;
}
```

#### Optional: auto-dismiss after a successful IQA

If you want the IQA screen to auto-dismiss on success, set `.iqaSuccessAutoDismissDelay(int)` in the builder. The OCR flow reads this value and starts an auto-dismiss timer when success arrives.

```java
@Override
public CardRecognizerCredentials getCredentials() {
    CardRecognizerCredentials credentials = new CardRecognizerCredentials.Builder()
        ...
        .iqaEnabled(true)
        .iqaSuccessAutoDismissDelay(5)
        .build();
    return credentials;
}
```

### Implement the IQA Callback

A new mandatory callback must be added to the fragment to avoid application crashes. You’ll get a side (front/back) and a normalized IQAFeedback. These operations are handled internally and corresponding feedback will be shown to the user with IQA screens.

```java
@Override
public void onIqaResult(IQAFeedback iqaFeedback, Process process) {
    // Optional: handle IQA result per side
}
```

